# Intermediate CA certificates that the servers below OMIT from their own
# TLS handshake. Nine hosts carrying 24 probed source candidates answered
# "unable to get local issuer certificate" for exactly this reason, measured
# 2026-10-21: the leaf is signed by a public CA, the server simply does not
# send the middle of its chain, and neither OpenSSL nor libcurl will go and
# fetch it.
#
# WHY THIS PRESERVES VERIFICATION, in full.
#
# Every certificate in this file is an INTERMEDIATE -- signed by another CA,
# never self-signed. OpenSSL does not use X509_V_FLAG_PARTIAL_CHAIN here, so a
# chain is only accepted when it ends at a SELF-SIGNED certificate in the
# trusted store. Adding a non-self-signed certificate therefore cannot create a
# new trust anchor: it supplies a link that still has to be vouched for by a
# root that was already trusted. Each block below records the root the chain
# was measured to reach, and `tests/unit/test_catrust.c` fails the build if any
# certificate in this file is self-signed, is expired, or cannot be chained to
# the system store.
#
# This is NOT a place to put a certificate a host's chain needs because the
# chain is broken, a self-signed certificate, or a private CA. Each entry is
# fetched from the issuing CA's own AIA ("CA Issuers") URL, which is named in
# the leaf certificate the server itself served.
#
# Regenerate/extend: fetch the host's leaf, read its AIA CA-Issuers URL, fetch
# that, and verify `openssl verify -CAfile <system roots> -untrusted <it>
# <leaf>` says OK before adding it.

